  "worked_solution": "The period of $y=cos\u2061x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for any integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ncos\u2061x=cos\u2061(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=8$ into the right-hand-side gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos\u2061x=cos\u2061(x+16\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $cos\u2061x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ then $cos\u2061(x+16\\pi )=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$",
